#################################################################
#  v6.5.0 — It9 was overfilled and nothing said so.
#
#  The re-plan at v1.113.0 put two batch stories into It9 on top of
#  the three already there: 15 points against a capacity of 9. The
#  capacity clause passed, because it compares hasCommittedEffort
#  against hasCapacity and BOTH ARE ASSERTED. Adding stories does
#  not change the declared commitment.
#
#  Same defect as an ungrounded practice, one level along: a number
#  stated rather than derived, agreeing with another stated number,
#  and nothing comparing either to the work. It10 was caught last
#  release only because its DECLARED figure was raised; It9 was not,
#  because it was not.
#
#  CommittedEffortShape now derives the sum from the contents.
#  Cascaded rather than compressed: the batch stories move one box
#  later each, and It12 opens. The migration finishes 24 January
#  instead of 10 January — still inside the 31 January deadline,
#  and the honest date rather than the one that fitted.
#################################################################


fw:Obs_ReplanCascaded a backlog:MetricObservation ;
    rdfs:label "Batch plan after the It9 correction"@en ;
    backlog:observesMetric fw:Metric_Obj_TablesExported ;
    backlog:observationFor fw:Obj_TablesExported ;
    backlog:hasObservedValue 22.0 ;
    backlog:observedAt "2026-08-27T05:58:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    backlog:hasObservationMethod "Unchanged at 22: re-planning moves nothing, only delivery does. The plan now finishes 24 January rather than 10 January, because It9 was carrying 15 points against a capacity of 9 and two batch stories cascade forward. Still inside the 31 January deadline. Recorded because a plan that slips two weeks under correction and is not written down looks identical to one that never slipped." .

#################################################################
#  v6.6.0 — the calendar was fiction. Re-based on measurement.
#
#  OWNER FINDING, and it is exact. Measured from the publish
#  commits: It7 took 32 minutes and It8 took 28. The register
#  declared FOURTEEN DAYS each and scheduled the remaining work for
#  November through January, while the day was the 27th of August.
#  Overstatement: 667 times.
#
#  Nothing caught it. iterationStart and iterationEnd are asserted
#  dateTimes and no clause compared them to anything that happened
#  — the same defect as committed effort, one release earlier: a
#  number stated rather than derived.
#
#  Re-based: the two closed iterations carry their MEASURED
#  durations and their real commit-bounded spans. The four open
#  iterations run thirty minutes each from now, which is the mean
#  of what the two closed ones actually took. Objective deadline
#  moves from 31 January to 08:00 today, and the checkpoints with
#  it — the whole migration is about two hours of work, not five
#  months.
#################################################################

fw:Obs_MeasuredCadence a backlog:MetricObservation ;
    rdfs:label "Measured iteration cadence"@en ;
    backlog:observesMetric fw:Metric_Obj_TablesExported ;
    backlog:observationFor fw:Obj_TablesExported ;
    backlog:hasObservedValue 22.0 ;
    backlog:observedAt "2026-08-27T05:55:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    backlog:hasObservationMethod "Count unchanged at 22 — re-basing the calendar moves no tables. What changed is the schedule: measured from the publish commits, a nine-point iteration takes 30 minutes on average, 3.4 minutes per point. The declared fourteen days was 667 times too long and the dates sat five months in the future. Four remaining iterations at 30 minutes finish about two hours from now rather than on 24 January. Recorded because a plan re-based this far without saying so would look like the original plan succeeding." .

#  It1 and It2 predate any measurement. Their durations are
#  recorded as UNMEASURABLE rather than invented: they closed
#  before this framework tracked publish commits per iteration, and
#  writing a plausible figure now would be exactly the fiction this
#  release removes.
fw:It1 backlog:hasObservedDuration 0.0 ;
    backlog:hasDurationSource "UNMEASURABLE: closed before per-iteration publish commits were tracked. Zero is recorded as a null, not as a claim that the iteration took no time — and it is visible as an outlier rather than hidden as a plausible guess." .
fw:It2 backlog:hasObservedDuration 0.0 ;
    backlog:hasDurationSource "UNMEASURABLE: as It1." .

#  The OntoQA metrics, with population sensitivity declared.
#  Exported from the quality assessor's python tuple.
fw:QM_ClassRichness a backlog:QualityMetric ;
    backlog:hasMetricName "ClassRichness" ; backlog:isPopulationSensitive true .
fw:QM_AveragePopulation a backlog:QualityMetric ;
    backlog:hasMetricName "AveragePopulation" ; backlog:isPopulationSensitive true .
fw:QM_RelationshipRichness a backlog:QualityMetric ;
    backlog:hasMetricName "RelationshipRichness" ; backlog:isPopulationSensitive false .
fw:QM_AttributeRichness a backlog:QualityMetric ;
    backlog:hasMetricName "AttributeRichness" ; backlog:isPopulationSensitive false .
fw:QM_InheritanceRichness a backlog:QualityMetric ;
    backlog:hasMetricName "InheritanceRichness" ; backlog:isPopulationSensitive false .

#################################################################
#  It9 delivered. Three stories, three findings.
#################################################################

fw:Ev_It9 a backlog:TestEvidence ;
    backlog:hasTestSpec "03-tooling/backlog_script_decision_audit_v1_0_0.py and backlog_standard_row_check_v1_0_0.py" ;
    backlog:hasTestId "It9" ;
    backlog:evidenceVerified true ;
    backlog:verifiedAt "2026-08-27T06:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    backlog:hasVerificationMethod "Script audit: 20 scripts scanned for the SHAPE of a decision rather than a list of known offenders — a list goes stale the moment someone writes a new one. Two findings, both the same classification written twice: which OntoQA metrics respond to population. Exported as QualityMetric with isPopulationSensitive, and the assessor now reads it and fails loudly without it. Audit now reports 0. Standard rows: 187 rows, 124 naming a TBox term, 0 naming a term the TBox lacks, 21 whose first cell is a CLAIM rather than an identifier. Those 21 are recorded as a known population and NOT excluded by a longer pattern list, because suppressing them would make the checker agree with the document by construction." ;
    backlog:verifiedByTool "backlog_script_decision_audit + backlog_standard_row_check" ;
    backlog:attestsCriterion fw:AC_EP_ScriptDecisions , fw:AC_EP_ScriptDecisions_S1 ,
        fw:AC_EP_StandardRows , fw:AC_EP_StandardRows_S1 , fw:AC_EP_StandardRows_S2 .

fw:Obs_CodeDecisionsAfterIt9 a backlog:MetricObservation ;
    backlog:observesMetric fw:Metric_Obj_CodeDecisions ;
    backlog:observationFor fw:Obj_CodeDecisions ;
    backlog:hasObservedValue 0.0 ;
    backlog:observedAt "2026-08-27T06:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    backlog:hasObservationMethod "Counted by the shipped audit over 20 scripts: 0 decisions remain in code. Baseline 3, target 0, MET. The audit itself caught one false positive on its first clean run — it flagged a docstring QUOTING the code it had just caused to be removed — and was fixed to parse rather than grep, because an audit that cannot tell an explanation from a decision reports its own success as a failure." .

